WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.630
- We're here to have fun and make food for people.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.220
Hey, guys, I'm Antoni Porowski from Netflix's Queer Eye,

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:09.290
and I'm here to teach you how to make sartu di riso.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:13.620
I learned about this recipe on a treadmill

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:16.130
at 8:00 or 9:00 p.m. when I was at the gym,

00:00:16.130 --> 00:00:19.220
and I was watching one of my favorite people in the world,

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:21.700
Giada De Laurentiis, and she was making this.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:23.683
It's actually a Neapolitano classic.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:26.640
Doing 40 minutes of some kind of cardio, which is terrible,

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.550
if I'm watching a good show, an hour can go by very quickly,

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:30.383
and before you know it,

00:00:30.383 --> 00:00:32.240
you've burned 900 calories and you know exactly

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:33.470
what you wanna make for dinner that night.

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:35.370
So, it's kind of like a double whammy.

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:38.600
Bay leaves are so important, it's just a layer of depth.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:40.180
You'll never taste anything and be like,

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:42.680
'oh, yeah, this is really bay leafy right now.'

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:45.863
It just makes it taste like grandma, that's what it is.

00:00:47.544 --> 00:00:50.434
♪ Don't stress out, we're just makin' food ♪

00:00:50.434 --> 00:00:53.578
♪ We're not savin' lives ♪

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:57.410
Ted Allen taught me to put parmesan rinds in my sauces.

00:00:57.410 --> 00:00:58.400
You just bury it in,

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:01.463
and it's just gonna flavor it with its goodness.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:03.570
And even just 30 to 40 minutes of this,

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:05.290
and that's really all that you need.

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:08.403
We have our bay leaf, parmesan rind, and the basil,

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:10.360
which it kinda doesn't get much more Italian than that,

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:12.072
but those are the three things that you also wanna remember

00:01:12.072 --> 00:01:14.372
because we're gonna be removing those as well.

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:18.503
We're using Arborio rice, which is a very short grain,

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:21.900
and might I add, very accessible rice grain as well

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:23.750
that I don't think is used that often,

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:25.452
and people think that it's only for risotto.

00:01:25.452 --> 00:01:28.120
And we're gonna cook this the way you would regular rice.

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:29.620
It really couldn't be simpler.

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:30.773
That's the thing,

00:01:30.773 --> 00:01:32.420
with this dish I wanna show that you can make something

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:34.580
that looks so beautiful and complex,

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:36.345
but the essence of it is really...

00:01:36.345 --> 00:01:39.053
If you break it down, it's actually very simple,

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:40.380
and anybody can make it.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:42.530
Food was never really something

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:45.293
that I planned on pursuing in a professional capacity.

00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:47.230
I had toyed with the idea in the past,

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:48.690
but I had sort of other ideas

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:50.490
of what I thought my life should look like,

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:52.610
and because I think cooking is such an intimate thing,

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:54.250
it's something that I do on a personal level

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:56.860
in my relationship, for my friends, for my loved ones,

00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:58.850
that I never really associated it

00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:01.230
with sort of a public thing that I could actually

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:03.233
work on or make a career out of.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:05.270
My father and my mother would travel

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:06.280
and they would go all over the world

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:07.900
and they would come back with these amazing recipes

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:09.100
that they would try out on us.

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:10.070
The thing is, with my mother,

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:12.973
she's such a perfectionist in the kitchen, not unlike me.

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:16.403
I was actually never allowed to participate in the cooking,

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:17.650
and I'm the same way.

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:19.781
I don't let anybody help me with my prep or any of it.

00:02:19.781 --> 00:02:22.300
So, I kind of understand where she was coming from.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:24.200
So, I would sort of sit at the other end of the counter

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:25.935
and just watch her prepare everything,

00:02:25.935 --> 00:02:28.883
and I learned very much by studying it,

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:31.100
which I think is actually a really good way to go about it

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:33.200
because you really get to pay attention to the details.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:34.120
By actually watching it,

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:36.040
you get to see things that sometimes you won't learn

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:37.490
during a conventional lesson.

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:42.270
So, with the mozzarella here,

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:45.630
what I'm doing is by tearing it into really smaller strands

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:47.450
it's gonna be a lot easier for it to melt.

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:49.140
We want it to really be sort of melty

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:50.690
and oozey and have that effect.

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:53.250
So, this is perfect consistency,

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:55.963
it's beautifully reduced, it's thick.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:58.743
If you go with a little more rice on the bottom,

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:01.040
it's totally fine because when we flip it,

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:03.900
it's gonna have a nice solid ground

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:05.483
for the whole thing to sit on.

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:07.470
So, there's gonna be a little note here

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:08.876
that says let cool for 10 minutes.

00:03:08.876 --> 00:03:09.923
- Yeah.
- Great.

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:18.740
When Queer Eye, when we were wrapping our season,

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:21.030
on our last day I thought I had this vision

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:22.670
of how nice it would be to sort of prepare

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:23.930
a family meal for everybody.

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:25.783
I made three or four of these,

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:28.690
which was insane and took me a whole day to do,

00:03:28.690 --> 00:03:31.560
but when I get an idea in my head, I just can't get it out,

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:34.520
and I spent like 3/4 of a day in my kitchen

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:36.020
and then put them in the trunk

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:38.410
and then we brought them to the production offices

00:03:38.410 --> 00:03:39.497
after we had wrapped our last day,

00:03:39.497 --> 00:03:42.290
and the crew came back and we had these,

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:44.703
like three or four of these guys, ready.

00:03:45.983 --> 00:03:47.743
I just wanna eat this!

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:49.933
Guys, dig in.

00:03:49.999 --> 00:03:51.530
- [Crew Member] I'm gonna get a few beauty shots of that.

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:52.703
- [Antoni] I'm just having one more bite.

00:03:52.703 --> 00:03:55.203
(all talking)

00:03:56.770 --> 00:03:57.760
- [Andrew] Oh, yes!

00:03:58.613 --> 00:04:01.113
(jazzy music)

